ert operations  that had preceded the bay of pig's  invasion  the impetus came from the white house  and in particular from the president's  younger brother bobby kennedy  the u.s attorney general effectively  took personal control of the covert  campaign against castro after the bay of  pigs  following the replacement of dulles and  bissell by mccone and helms the cia  itself was in a much less bullish mood  regarding cuba  in november 1961 the cia and pentagon  jointly launched operation mongoose a 50  million dollar a year program that bobby  kennedy hoped would stir things up on in  the island with espionage sabotage and  general disorder by the following year a  cia base in oprah locker florida code  named jm wave was the agency's largest  outside langley  it employed 400 clandestine service  officers as well as thousands of cuban  exiles  it also ran as many as 400 front  companies throughout south florida and  managed the third largest navy in the  caribbean  maintaining secrecy  in such a huge operation was nearly  impossible as it had been before the bay  of pigs  the base was teeming with gun merchants  soldiers of fortune and cuban politicos  hoping to replace castro  its presence was an open secret in  florida  richard helms wanted mongoose to  generate intelligence as well as covert  action  so he set up a reception center at the  florida base to debrief thousands of  cubans still fleeing castro  thanks to mongoose the cia had a better  espionage network on the island than  before the bay of pigs  but as in earlier operations there was  also a lot of silliness such as  proposals for air drops of toilet paper  with portraits of castro and khrushchev  printed on alternate squares  and there were more assassination plots  many more  responding to castro's known fondness  for ocean diving one proposal involved  coating the cuban's diving suit with  deadly contaminants  another for planting a beautiful  seashell rigged with explosives near his  favorite diving beach  helms brought in a familiar face to  oversee the planning this was william  harvey the heavy drinking pistol-packing  ex-fbi man who would run the cia's  berlin station  in his new role harvey was introduced to  johnny rosselli uh the mobster had been  recruited to help the agency with  earlier attempts to eliminate castro  harvey and roselli got along famously  helms by the way didn't inform mccone  about the efforts to kill castro knowing  the director would object due to his  strong catholic faith  helms himself maintained reservations  believing that efforts to assassinate  foreign leaders invited the risk of  retaliation in kind  and none of it worked the only real  effect of operation mongoose was to make  castro even more suspicious of the  americans and to drive him further into  the arms of the soviets  defense secretary robert mcnamara said  later if i was a cuban and read the  evidence of covert american action  against their government  i would be quite ready to believe that  the us intended to mount an invasion  and it's worth adding that even though  jack and bobby kennedy's dislike of  castro was unique in its intensity the  cuban operations weren't an isolated cia  phenomenon  in relative terms the kennedy era saw  more covert ops than the eisenhower  years had especially in the western  hemisphere  during the early 60s the u.s engaged in  covert actions in the dominican republic  ecuador british guyana and brazil  and in efforts to support repressive  anti-communist governments in other  countries castro in looking around  might be forgiven for thinking that jack  kennedy was laying siege to latin  america  so in the summer of 1962 castro decided  to go along with khrushchev on deploying  nuclear missiles on cuba hoping that  doing so might deter the americans from  further aggression  to summarize  although the cia was not the driving  force  that would have been the kennedy  brothers  its anti-castro operations did  contribute to the siege mentality that  opened cuba up to soviet missiles  so  how was the agency's performance during  the rest of the cuban missile crisis  starting with the american discovery of  the missiles  well at the beginning not very good  agency analysts noted an increase of  soviet shipments to cuba in july 1962  and a u2 overflight photographed a new  surface-to-air missile site that august  but at this stage the cia along with the  rest of the us intelligence community  believed that soviet weapons on the  island were limited to the conventional  defensive kind  on september 19 1962 the agency stated  in a now notorious national intelligence  estimate that nothing in khrushchev's  previous behavior suggested he would do  something as reckless as deploying  offensive nukes so close to the united  states  meanwhile president kennedy ordered a  suspension of u-2 reconnaissance flights  over cuba fearing another incident like  the soviet shoot down of u.s pilot gary  powers in may 1960.  the cia was now in something akin to an  intelligence shutdown in cuba  however  one dissenting voice in washington  belonged to none other than the cia  director john mccone  that august and september he repeatedly  voiced a hunch that the soviets were  putting offensive weapons on cuba while  on his honeymoon in the south of france  mccone even sent the white house cables  to that effect  other national security experts were  skeptical attributing his suspicion of  khrushchev to his catholicism his  anti-communism and a lack of previous  government intelligence experience  still  reports were coming in from mongoose  field agents about mysterious movements  of unusually long soviet traders near  the northwestern cuban town of san  cristobal  on october 9th kennedy relented and  authorized a resumption of u2 flights  over cuba  now the cia's performance went from  below par to stella  the first reconnaissance mission flew at  dawn on october 14th  piloted by air force major richard d  heiser  the following day analysts in the cia's  national photographic interpretation  center examined the images heiser had  taken  they showed three missile sites around  san cristobal  subsequent youtube flights would reveal  three more sites near san cristobal and  two at guanaj  the american analysts compared these  missiles with photographs of mayday  parades in moscow and consulted  technical manuals provided to them by a  spy and soviet military intelligence  named oleg penkovsky  by the late afternoon of october 15th  they'd reached an alarming conclusion  the initial battery consisted of ss4  medium-range ballistic missiles capable  of carrying nuclear warheads 300 miles  as far as washington  richard helms took the news to bobby  kennedy early the next morning his reply  damn it all to hell and back  to sum up the cia's performance in the  run-up to the crisis agency officials  with the notable exception of john  mccone failed to predict the soviet  deployment on cuba  but then they made up for their initial  error by skillfully using technological  and human intelligence to detect the  missiles  some historians have even forgiven the  cia analyst their earlier era given that  khrushchev's actions were so out of  character  in any case thanks to the cia and in  particular the much maligned richard  bissell john kennedy now had some  valuable intelligence and time in which  to craft a response before moscow was  aware that its scheme had been  discovered  on october 16 1962  kennedy assembled the executive  committee of the national security  council this was the group known as xcom  consisting of the so-called wise men of  foreign policy who would advise the  president on how to respond  kennedy's first instinct was to order up  airstrikes on the missile sites  in the course of x-com discussions  however the president backed away from  the military option fearing that it  might start a full-scale nuclear war  in a national tv address on october 22nd  kennedy demanded that the soviets  withdraw the missiles  and he announced that he was imposing a  naval quarantine on cuba to prevent any  more soviet shipments from reaching it  the world held its breath as soviet  ships sailed toward the quarantine line  but on october 24th they began turning  back  when xcom learned this secretary of  state dean rusk lent over the national  security adviser george bundy and said  we are eyeball to eyeball and i think  the other fellow just blinked  kennedy's strategy was working  but the crisis wasn't over yet missiles  remained on cuba  now the cia's performance during the  crisis wasn't perfect  agency analysts substantially  underestimated the number of soviet  troops on cuba  reckoning a total of eight thousand to  ten thousand when the true figure was  nearer to forty three 000.  they also missed evidence of tactical  nuclear weapons on the island including  some missiles aimed at the u.s naval  base in guantanamo bay  had jfk decided on air strikes followed  by an invasion american forces would  have met with much more resistance than  the cia had predicted and the presence  of tactical nukes spelled the  possibility of a rapid escalation to  all-out nuclear war  overall though the cia performed well  mccone's daily briefings to xcom on the  latest intelligence developments were  effective  and he was able to be supported by the  head of the national photographic  interpretation center a man named art  lundell  further agency analysts atone for the  erroneous national intelligence estimate  of september 19th by providing two  updated estimates on october 20th about  possible u.s actions and likely soviet  responses  these structured much of xcom's  subsequent debate  one particularly gratifying moment came  after president kennedy's october 22nd  tv address  when the soviet representative to the  united nations security council valerian  zorin demanded evidence of the missile's  existence in response u.s representative  adlai stevenson produced a series of u-2  and lower-level reconnaissance  photographs making the soviets squirm in  his seat  here was the cia doing what it was  founded to do providing u.s leaders with  central intelligence  still i don't want to create the  impression that the kennedy  administration was entirely in control  of events  recent historical scholarship argues  that the cuban missile crisis was more  dangerous than the world knew at the  time  even as american and soviet leaders  worked over the weekend of october 27th  and 28th toward a resolution that would  bring the 13-day crisis to an end  a new series of incidents could have  tipped the super powers into full-scale  conflict  first on the night of october 26th to  27th a u-2 plane based in alaska strayed  into soviet airspace  the pilot captain chuck monsby was on a  routine mission collecting air samples  to monitor soviet nuclear tests  but he became confused by the aurora  borealis the northern lights  while navigating by the stars and he  drifted off course  soviet mig scrambled to intercept the u2  and u.s fighters took off to rescue it  u.s forces were in a state of defcon 2  nuclear alert and american planes were  carrying air-to-air missiles with  low-yield nuclear warheads and no  electronic locks disaster loomed it was  possible that the soviets might  interpret this as a reconnaissance  mission preparing for a u.s first strike  as american nuclear war planning  dictated  fortunately u.s authorities guided  maltsby back to base before the opposing  fighter planes engaged  then  on october 27th black saturday as it  became known another u2 was shot down by  a soviet surface-to-air missile while  overflying cuba  the pilot major rudolf anderson have  returned from previous u2 missions with  some of the earliest photographs of the  cuban missile sites  now it would be photographs of his three  children that the cubans recovered next  to his dead body  the white house considered bombing the  surface-to-air missile battery that  launched the attack  but the decision had been that of a  local soviet commander acting on his own  initiative and not a kremlin order  still as the tension of black saturday  mounted there was a growing sense that  events were spinning out of control  the most dangerous incident took place  not in the sky but in the ocean  the u.s navy was pursuing four  submarines of the soviet northern fleet  of cuba dropping hand grenades and depth  charges to get them to surface  what the navy didn't know was that in  addition to conventional armaments each  submarine was carrying a nuclear torpedo  with a yield equal to the bomb dropped  on hiroshima  several of the soviet submarine captains  interpreted the detonations as attacks  rather than warnings  one submariner compared the blast to  sledgehammers pounding on a metal barrel  conditions were particularly desperate  aboard submarine b-59  where rising temperatures and carbon  dioxide levels caused men to faint  captain valentin savitsky ordered his  crew to arm the nuclear torpedo he said  we're gonna blast them now we will die  but we will sink them all  other officers managed to calm him and  persuaded him to surface  of all the incidents on black saturday  perhaps this one came closest to  starting world war iii  october 27th the most dangerous day of  the cuban missile crisis was also  crucial to its resolution khrushchev  sent kennedy an impassioned letter  voicing his fear of nuclear war and  offering to withdraw the missiles in  return for a pledge that the united  states would not invade cuba  but only hours later the soviet premier  followed up with a second letter  demanding that the united states remove  some of its nuclear armory near the  soviet union  these were the jupiter missiles in  turkey  khrushchev's offer was the subject of  heated debate at xcom where members were  exhausted after nearly two weeks of high  stress  john mccone who'd generally taken a hard  line in dealing with the soviets now  advised accepting the offer pointing out  that the jupiter missiles were  technologically obsolescent anyway  that evening bobby kennedy met secretly  with the soviet ambassador  anatoly dobrinin and agreed to the terms  in return for the soviets dismantling  their missiles on the island the united  states would promise publicly to not  invade cuba and secretly remove its own  jupiter missiles  by the way this was all done without  consulting fidel castro who was furious  with the soviets as well as with the  americans  the following morning radio moscow  announced that the cuban missiles were  being returned to the soviet union  jfk told an aide  i feel like a new man now thank god it's  all over  the cia had done well during the crisis  and would continue to perform  effectively afterwards in monitoring and  verifying the soviet removal of the  missiles  but it got surprisingly little thanks  for its contributions the kennedys had  still not forgotten the bay of pigs  fiasco nor the subsequent failures of  operation mongoose which arguably helped  cause the crisis in the first place  and john mccombe perhaps spent too much  time reminding people that he was the  only us official to have predicted  trouble  this was true but it was also  embarrassing to the president who in  march 1963 could be heard saying he's a  real bastard that john mccomb  and even now the kennedys couldn't give  up their castro obsession  in june 1963 the national security  council approved a new program of  sabotage after the cuban leader returned  from a trip to moscow  the assassination plotting resumed as  well this time under the charge of the  far east hand desmond fitzgerald who was  a throwback to the dulles era of  gentleman adventurers  the planning now focused on a  disaffected cuban government official  named rolando kubala  on november 22nd cia officers met with q  baylor in paris and provided him with a  ballpoint pen  rigged with a poisonous hypodermic  needle to use on fidel castro  this was also the day of john kennedy's  assassination in dallas  conspiracy theories that there was a  direct connection between the repeated  cia attempts to kill castro and  president kennedy's death  have never been proven  but the suspicions would haunt the  agency for years to come  and further tarnish its reputation in  washington  [Music]  you

